---
name: fix-task
version: 1.1.0
description: lead post-triage fix assignment template for codex-orchestration QA findings.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - phase
  - sprint_doc
  - branch
  - worktree_path
  - pr_target
  - description
  - finding_ids
  - triage_records
  - required_fixes
  - acceptance_criteria
  - references
optional_variables:
  - sprint_id
  - lead
  - cc
  - validation_requirements
  - dependency_relations
  - assignee
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  validation_requirements: ""
  dependency_relations: "- See authoritative sprint doc."
  assignee: "cobs"
metadata:
  type: fix-task
  tags:
    - stage:fix
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: phase
      variable: phase
    state: fix-start
    stage: fix
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" phase="{{ phase }}" assignee="{{ assignee }}" mode="fix">
  <description>{{ description }}</description>

  <branch>{{ branch }}</branch>
  <worktree>{{ worktree_path }}</worktree>
  <pr-target>{{ pr_target }}</pr-target>
  <stack-discipline>`{{ branch }}` is a layer cut from the current top of the phase stack; `{{ pr_target }}` is the layer directly below it. Work only on this layer. Never edit, push to, rebase, or open a PR against any layer below, and never wait for a lower layer's QA or CI. If `{{ pr_target }}` gains commits while you work, run `git fetch origin &amp;&amp; git merge origin/{{ pr_target }}` once at task start and once before the final push; the parent wins where copies differ. Once this task closes the layer is frozen: every later finding against it is fixed on a new layer cut from the top of the stack, never here.</stack-discipline>
  <authoritative-sprint-doc>{{ sprint_doc }}</authoritative-sprint-doc>

  <dependency-relations>
{{ dependency_relations }}
  </dependency-relations>

  <finding-ids>
{{ finding_ids }}
  </finding-ids>

  <triage-records>
{{ triage_records }}
  </triage-records>

  <required-fixes>
{{ required_fixes }}
  </required-fixes>

  <acceptance-criteria>
{{ acceptance_criteria }}
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <validation-requirements>
{{ validation_requirements }}
  </validation-requirements>

  <references>
{{ references }}
  </references>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a">Start the task immediately with `atm task start {{ task_id }} "&lt;one line: what you will do first&gt;"`, then begin executing it in the same work session unless blocked.</step>
    <step id="b">Read `{{ sprint_doc }}` before editing. Treat it as the authoritative source for sprint scope, retained requirements, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria. If this assignment summary and the sprint doc differ, the sprint doc wins and the mismatch must be reported.</step>
    <step id="c">Update the assigned worktree from the target branch before editing. Include all earlier-sprint fixes already merged forward to that branch.</step>
    <step id="c1">Honor the `&lt;dependency-relations&gt;` block above: must-follow merge-forward trigger is parent dev push, not QA; merge parent → child before every round. PR-completion trigger is parent PR merge first. Stop if a parallel-safe relation intersects.</step>
    <step id="d">Read the triage records and findings report. Create `{{ worktree_path }}/.atm-task-lists/{{ task_id }}.md` listing every assigned fix with separate implementation and verification checkboxes. Keep this list local to the worktree.</step>
    <step id="d2">Before fixing each finding, verify the defect it describes is still actually present at the cited file:line in this worktree's current state. If a finding does not reproduce — already fixed, superseded by other work, or the described code path no longer exists — refuse to perform speculative or invented work to "address" it. Do not edit unrelated code in its name. Note it in your push/validation report as `not-reproducible` with the file:line you checked instead, and skip it. This applies per-finding, not to the whole task — fix every finding that does reproduce as normal.</step>
    <step id="e">First pass: loop over the entire list and implement every reproducible fix, including all affected occurrences. Leave unresolved items open.</step>
    <step id="e2">Second pass: revisit every item and verify the fix in the actual files and its test/check evidence. Record that evidence; repair and recheck failures. Do not send an incomplete set to QA.</step>
    <step id="f">As soon as the branch is pushable, commit and push immediately. Send branch name, commit hash, and task-list path to the assigner with plain `atm send {{ lead }} --stdin`. Distinguish a progress push from a completed two-pass handoff; never close the task on a push. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="f1">Before assigning QA, `{{ lead }}` checks every assigned fix against the list, pushed diff, and cited evidence. Return incomplete work through the fix template. This completeness check does not replace independent QA.</step>
    <step id="g">Run the required validation after the push report. While it is FAIL, keep fixing and report progress with plain `atm send`. When it is PASS, close the task with the validation report as the body: `atm task close {{ task_id }} completed --stdin &lt;&lt;'EOF' … EOF` — PASS plus the gate output lines and, per finding, fixed or `not-reproducible` with the file:line checked. Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment stays queued and never starts until `{{ task_id }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it. Use `atm task close {{ task_id }} refused "&lt;reason&gt;"` only when the whole assignment cannot be done. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
{% if triage_records and triage_records|trim %}    <step id="g1">Triage records are attached to this assignment. Use the `/closing-triage` skill to work them to zero before reporting: `{{ branch }}` is a stacked layer no phase structure declares, so its query runs as `python3 .claude/skills/closing-triage/scripts/query_open_findings.py --branch {{ branch }}{% if sprint_id %} --sprint {{ sprint_id }}{% endif %} --phase {{ phase }} --json` from your worktree. The finding ids it returns are the full scope of this assignment; fix every one in this pass.</step>
{% endif %}    <step id="h">After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.</step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
